{\em L} & A $\to$ A & An R-linear map\\
{\em b} & R & Generator of an orbit\\
{\em n} & Integer & Size of the orbit\\
$\pi$ & (A, Integer) $\to$ R & Coefficient function\\
$\sigma$ & A $\to$ Integer & Size function\\
}
\Descr{
\name($[b_1,\dots,b_n]$,$\pi$,$\nu$) returns the matrix of the subspace
generated by $[b_1,\dots,b_n]$, but the number of rows is set to $m$ in the
first case, while
\name(L,$[b_1,\dots,b_n]$,$\pi$,$\nu$) returns the matrix of the image
of the subspace generated by $[b_1,\dots,b_n]$ under L, and
\name(L, b, n, $\pi$, $\nu$) returns the matrix of the subspace generated
by $[b, L b, L^2 b, \dots, L^{n-1} b]$.}
\Remarks{
A module basis is implicitely given by the coefficient
function $\pi$, where $\pi(a,i)$ returns the \Th{i} coefficient of a.
The size function $\nu$ returns an upper bound on the number of basis
elements needed, \ie~$\pi(a,i) = 0$ for $i > \nu(a)$.
It is assumed for $\pi$ and $\nu$ that the first basis element has
index 1, not 0. This implies that $\nu(a) > 0$ for any $a \ne 0$.
}
